      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\), we can use the sum-to-product identities for sine and cosine. Specifically, we know that:\n\n\\[\n\\sin A \\cos B - \\cos A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\sin (A + B) - \\sin (A - B)]\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\cos A \\cos B + \\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A - B) + \\cos (A + B)]\n\\]\n\nIn our case, let \\(A = 2x\\) and \\(B = 3x\\). Applying the sum-to-product identities:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\cos 3x - \\cos 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\sin (2x + 3x) - \\sin (2x - 3x)]\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\cos 3x + \\cos 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (2x - 3x) + \\cos (2x + 3x)]\n\\]\n\nThis translates to:\n\n\\[\n\\sin (2x + 3x) - \\sin (2x - 3x) = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 5x\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\cos (2x - 3x) + \\cos (2x + 3x) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos (-x)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying further:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 5x - \\sin (-5x) = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 5x\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin (-x) = -\\sin x\\), this becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 5x + \\sin 5x = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 5x\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2 \\sin 5x = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 5x\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 2 to clear the fractions:\n\n\\[\n4 \\sin 5x = \\sin 5x\n\\]\n\n\\[\n3 \\sin 5x = 0\n\\]\n\nThis gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 5x = 0\n\\]\n\nThe angles whose sine is zero are:\n\n\\[\n5x = 180^\\circ k \\quad \\text{for integer} \\ k\n\\]\n\nConsidering the smallest positive angle \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n5x = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180^\\circ}{5} = 36^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{36}\n\\]",
