      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "First, we simplify the given equation using the product-to-sum formulas:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x &= \\cos 2x \\cos 3x \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} (\\underbrace{-\\cos(x-x))}_{-(\\cos(2x)}) &= \\frac{1}{2} (\\cos(6x-x)) \\\\\n\\cos(x) &= \\cos(5x).\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nNow, we consider two cases: $\\cos(x) = \\cos(5x)$ and $\\cos(x) = -\\cos(5x)$.\n\nFirst, using $\\cos(x) = \\cos(5x) \\Rightarrow x=n \\cdot 360^{\\circ}, \\quad n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ also we have $2=k \\cdot 360^{\\circ}, \\quad k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. This gives $k=1$ and $x=2*1=2$ degrees.\n\nIf we use $\\cos(x)=-\\cos(5x)$, we can only use this formula for $\\cos(x)$ at $x=360*60^{\\circ}=2160^{\\circ}$. But this is just another multiple of the first case of the solution.\n\nSo, the only solution is $\\boxed{2^{}^{th}}$ degree.",
